e odyssey of a young boy whose  parents try to teach him he can be  invisible the idea of invisibility keeps  you safe and this is kind of rooted in  my own growing i was very introverted i  got bullied a fair amount and i always  wanted to just disappear like if i can  just disappear from this moment i'll be  okay the problem with that is when you  disappear you also lose your voice you  lose your presence you use your ability  to speak for yourself that is the  drawback of it hell of a book is both  absurd and alarming with searing  descriptions of what life can be like as  a black american  jumping back and forth between matt's  less life and death personal  fascinations  including a legendary actor you know for  movies like raising arizona leaving las  vegas  and the rock how in the name of zeus's  did you get out of yourself i want to  talk about nicholas cage we can talk  about nick cage all day  [Laughter]  why  because he's an amazing actor the beauty  of nick cage is he understands how the  world sees him and he controls that  narrative you bet i do anytime you can  know how someone else sees you and  really understand it i think that's a  very insightful powerful thing i would  like to dedicate this award to all the  other mad kids  to all the outsiders the weirdos the  bullied  do you want in this book to make people  think do you want to make them mad  all of the above i want to make people  think i want people to laugh i want to  make them really angry and sad  the black experience in particular is a  myriad of things it is not just the  slave narrative and the civil rights  narrative and the inner city narrative  and it is those things but we are also  absurdists we are also comical people we  like film noir and we want to make jokes  about being on book tour as we also want  to be seen as people we want to be able  to interact with police officers and  authority figures without worrying that  our skin may be the thing that gets us  killed what's more fulfilling for you  writing  fantasy fiction or  i mean writing stories about issues that  are life and death all of it quite  frankly like both sides have their own  way of speaking like i grew up as the  nerd who read you know beowulf and the  odyssey and lord of the flies and you  know lord of the rings and all those  kinds of books  and so part of the challenge of being a  minority writer is  you're almost always expected to write  about your minority lens how many  imaginations are we losing to this idea  of like they can only write about their  blackness or they can only write about  their queerness or whatever that thing  is when you're a minority writer you're  not given the freedom to stretch out and  spend years just  writing whatever fantasy it does not  relate to that i mean it's very hard to  do  matt's array of interests are tough to  pin down  he practices jiu-jitsu and is a creative  writing professor at the university of  north carolina wilmington what do you  tell kids these days i tell kids mainly  like there's a reality to being a writer  that the idea the image they have of  writing and the image they have of being  a writer probably is very false and very  skewed  but perhaps more than anything jason  motz is a homebody he spent his entire  life in a town with a population of 600.  you grew up in bolton yes went to school  here at unc wilmington for undergraduate  and graduates yes still live in bolton  yes  never left bolton no  why i like small town life like i've  visited a lot of cities and i've i've  been a lot of places but at the end of  the day  i live on land that is almost four  generations of mod family land like  there is a depth and a richness and  living in this place where you know not  only the people beside you but you know  their children and their grandparents  because the south is a place that is  very much about roots and about the past  and so living in this place where i've  lived i've learned to connect with the  past  today the town of bolton has produced a  singular writer who refuses to be boxed  in  and proves it again with yes  a hell of a book  in that tiny town he has no wi-fi or  broadband access wow at his home so i  actually asked him i said do you  actually like that because sometimes  writers you know don't be distracted he  said no it's a pain  um he would prefer it he is though now  that the pandemic is in a different  phase going to be able to tour a little  bit and we got to go the dish is next  folks the dish is next  [Music]  i love when he said the idea that the  students don't get the image of a writer  that they think it's one thing and then  it's another because you do think about  it you talked about it with minority  writers that there's this expectation  that you can only write about  your experience i i when people always  say what's your favorite kind of story  to do i loved his answer because it's  mine it's i love all stories good  stories yeah  good stories it just feels like he  shouldn't been he shouldn't be boxed  into writing any particular sort and  he's jumped around now the first three  were sort of fantasy fiction yeah this  is a change  um but just the way he sort of melds  these are a lot of complicated subjects  again going from as we said absurd to  alarming not easy he really does it  beautifully  um in this book and you know he was  shocked when it won national book award  but after reading it i mean  delve in he also when he was talking  about being a kid and reading and you  know reading beowulf reading  right whatever it may be you think about  kids now and i think about this a lot  with cell phones and everything else  you want them to read because that's  what helps develop that imagination and  that mind in that way yeah no i  my daughter  said mom can you please take me to the  bookstore i i love you out of nowhere  and i was thrilled and she picked up a  book she's reading more now and i think  we have something to do with that and  the book well the bookstore is where you  make those discoveries right we found  jason mott's book i found jason mott's  book at a bookstore i said this looks  interesting i read it said it's great  let's do it does not feel good yeah

